steps.message:
Done. Built with TDD — every test was written first and watched fail before the code that makes it pass.

**`src/utils.js`** — `isValidEmail(email)`:
```js
function isValidEmail(email) {
  if (typeof email !== 'string') {
    return false;
  }

  const at = email.indexOf('@');
  if (at <= 0) {
    return false;
  }
  return email.slice(at + 1).includes('.');
}
```

**`src/utils.test.js`** — 7 tests, all passing:
- accepts `user@example.com`
- rejects no `@` (`user.example.com`)
- rejects nothing before `@` (`@example.com`)
- rejects domain with no dot (`user@example`)
- rejects when the only dot is in the local part (`first.last@example`) — catches the common bug of checking the whole string for a dot
- rejects empty string
- rejects `undefined`/`null`/number instead of throwing

**Cycles run:** 5 red-green rounds. Each new test failed for the right reason first (`isValidEmail is not a function`, then assertion mismatches, then a `TypeError` on non-string input), and each got the minimal code to pass.

One caveat worth flagging: the empty-string test passed the moment I wrote it — the `at <= 0` check from the previous cycle already covered it. It's a regression guard, not a test that drove code.

**Also changed:** added `"test": "node --test src/"` to `package.json`. The repo had no test framework, so I used Node's built-in runner (v26.8.2) — zero new dependencies, matching the project's dependency-free CommonJS style.

Two things beyond your spec that I decided rather than asked about, both easy to reverse:
- **Non-string input returns `false`** rather than throwing. Without it, `isValidEmail(undefined)` crashes on a form field that was never filled in. Say the word if you'd prefer it throw.
- The validation is **structural only**. It accepts things like `user@.com` and `a@b.c`, and rejects valid-but-exotic addresses. That's what the four rules you gave specify; anything stricter is a regex arms race that tends to reject real addresses. Tell me if you want tighter domain rules and I'll drive them in the same way.

I haven't committed anything.
